"Almost all graphs are determined by their generalized spectrum (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.11047)\nciteturn5academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Date: Thu Oct  8 01:00:44 2026\n\nHaemers' conjecture for the generalized spectrum asserts that almost all graphs are determined by their generalized spectrum, that is, for almost all graphs $G$, every graph with the same spectrum as $G$ whose complement has the same spectrum as the complement of $G$ is isomorphic to $G$. ... The proof combines a local theory of the denominators of the rational orthogonal matrices that relate generalized cospectral graphs, Fourier analysis of random symmetric matrices over finite rings, Seidel switching, inverse Littlewood--Offord theory over finite fields, rank estimates for random symmetric matrices modulo primes, and a study of the rational factors of the characteristic polynomial of a random symmetric $\\pm1$ matrix with zero diagonal.\nTitle: Almost all graphs are determined by their generalized spectrum\nAuthors: Ziqing Xiang\nDate: Thu Oct  8 01:00:44 2026\n\nHaemers' conjecture for the generalized spectrum asserts that almost all graphs are determined by their generalized spectrum, that is, for almost all graphs $G$, every graph with the same spectrum as $G$ whose complement has the same spectrum as the complement of $G$ is isomorphic to $G$. We prove this conjecture by showing that the random graph $G(n,1/2)$ is determined by its generalized spectrum with probability tending to one. The proof combines a local theory of the denominators of the rational orthogonal matrices that relate generalized cospectral graphs, Fourier analysis of random symmetric matrices over finite rings, Seidel switching, inverse Littlewood--Offord theory over finite fields, rank estimates for random symmetric matrices modulo primes, and a study of the rational factors of the characteristic polynomial of a random symmetric $\\pm1$ matrix with zero diagonal.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe number \\(R(n)\\) also has an interpretation in terms of degrees of irreducible symmetric group characters. This is a comment of Regev given on the OEIS entry without proof, so we present a proof of a refinement here: (https://pages.mtu.edu/~djhemmer/papers/EJCRiordan.pdf)\nciteturn5search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; * Given a standard tableau of shape \\((k,k,1^m)\\), we describe how to construct a corresponding Riordan path. ... In a 2023 post on Math-Overflow [Amd23], Amdeberhan raised the following intriguing question about certain weighted sums of irreducible characters for symmetric groups, with columns corresponding to \\(\\mathcal{R}_N(2m)\\) or \\(\\mathcal{R}_N^c(2m)\\) and rows corresponding to \\(\\mathrm{Ev}(\\lambda)\\).\n\nTHE ELECTRONIC JOURNAL OF COMBINATORICS 33(1) (2026), #P1.57\nFor example, let \\(k=m=3\\) and\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nA Young tableau example labeled \\(T=\\) with shape \\((3,3,1^3)\\): first row entries \\(1,2,7\\); second row entries \\(3,5,8\\); first column below with \\(4,6,9\\). Equation number \\((1.6)\\) appears to the right.\n</visual_element>\n\nFilling in \\(U\\)s in positions \\(1,2,7\\) and \\(D\\) in the last position, we obtain \\(UU????U?D\\). The remaining entries are \\(\\{4,5,6,8,9\\}\\), where red denotes the entries in the final \\(m\\) rows. Thus, we get a corresponding sequence \\(FDFDFF\\), which we use to replace the question marks, arriving at \\(UUFDFDUFD\\). It is straightforward to verify the resulting sequence is Riordan and to invert the map. \\(\\square\\)\n\nSumming over all \\(m\\), an immediate corollary is the observation of Regev:\n\n**Corollary 3.** *The number of Riordan paths of length \\(n\\) is:*\n\n\\[\nR(n)=\\sum_{k=1}^{\\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor} f^{(k,k,1^{\\,n-2k})}.\n\\qquad (1.7)\n\\]\n\n## 2  Amdeberhan’s Question\n\nFor a partition \\(\\lambda\\) with part frequencies \\((1^{m_1},2^{m_2},\\ldots,n^{m_n})\\), we denote with\n\n\\[\nz_\\lambda=\\prod_{j=1}^{n} j^{m_j} m_j!\n\\]\n\nthe size of the centralizer of a permutation with cycle type \\(\\lambda\\). In a 2023 post on Math-Overflow [Amd23], Amdeberhan raised the following intriguing question about certain weighted sums of irreducible characters for symmetric groups, with columns corresponding to \\(\\mathcal{R}_N(2m)\\) or \\(\\mathcal{R}_N^c(2m)\\) and rows corresponding to \\(\\mathrm{Ev}(\\lambda)\\).\n\nTHE ELECTRONIC JOURNAL OF COMBINATORICS 33(1) (2026), #P1.57\n\n3--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHaemers Conjecture -- from Wolfram MathWorld (https://mathworld.wolfram.com/HaemersConjecture.html)\nciteturn5search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; In general, it is difficult to determine if a graph is determined by spectrum. van Dam and Haemers (van Dam and Haemers 2002, Haemers 2016) noted that it is conceivable that almost all graphs have this property, i.e., that the fraction of graphs on Image: n vertices with a cospectral mate tends to zero as Image: n tends to infinity, an assertion sometimes known as Haemer's conjecture (Brouwer and Spence 2009, Wang and Wang 2024).\n\n# Haemers Conjecture\n\n* * *\n\nIn general, it is difficult to determine if a graph is determined by spectrum. van Dam and Haemers (van Dam and Haemers 2002, Haemers 2016) noted that it is conceivable that almost all graphs have this property, i.e., that the fraction of graphs on Image: n vertices with a cospectral mate tends to zero as Image: n tends to infinity, an assertion sometimes known as Haemer's conjecture (Brouwer and Spence 2009, Wang and Wang 2024).\n\n## See also\n\nCospectral Graphs, Determined by Spectrum, Graph Spectrum\n\n## References\n\nBrouwer, A. E. and Spence, E. \"Cospectral Graphs on 12 Vertices.\" Elect. J. Combin., Vol. 16, No. 1, 2009. https://doi.org/10.37236/258.Haemers, W. H. \"Are Almost All Graphs Determined by Their Spectrum?\" Not. S. Afr. Math. Soc. 47, 42-45, 2016.van Dam, E. R. and Haemers, W. H. \"Which Graphs Are Determined by Their Spectrum?\" Lin. Algebra Appl. 373, 139-162, 2003.Wang, W. and Wang, W. \"Haemers' Conjecture: An Algorithmic Perspective.\" Experimental Math., 10 Apr 2024.\n\n## Cite this as:\n\nWeisstein, Eric W. \"Haemers Conjecture.\" From MathWorld--A Wolfram Resource. https://mathworld.wolfram.com/HaemersConjecture.html--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDennis Stanton - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/author/dennis-stanton)\nciteturn5search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Reflection factorizations of Singer cycles\nJoel Brewster Lewis, Victor Reiner, Dennis Stanton\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nReflection factorizations of Singer cycles\n\nPublished in J. Alg. Comb. 40, issue 3, Nov. 2014, pp. 663-691 • View Publication • BIB\n\nThe number of shortest factorizations into reflections for a Singer cycle in GL_n(F_q) is shown to be (q^n-1)^(n - 1). Formulas counting factorizations of any length, and counting those with reflections of fixed conjugacy classes are also given. The method is a standard character-theory technique, requiring the compilation of irreducible character values for Singer cycles, semisimple reflections, and transvections. The results suggest several open problems and questions, which are discussed at the end.\n\nirreducible character--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCereceda's Conjecture | ProofAtlas (https://www.proofatlas.ai/collaboration/cerecedas-conjecture/)\nciteturn5search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Image: A dark six-vertex path uses coral, teal, split coral-and-ivory, plum, cyan, and amber disks, with an incomplete halo around the split focal disk.For each fixed degeneracy d and palettes of at least d+2 colors, Cereceda's conjecture asks for quadratic-length paths between proper colorings using one-vertex changes.On this pageProblem ... Mixing graph colourings original source · Luis Cereceda · London School of Economics and Political Science · 2007 · accessed Aug 26, 2026 ... A polynomial version of Cereceda's conjecture peer reviewed result · Nicolas Bousquet, Marc Heinrich · Journal of Combinatorial Theory, Series B · 2022 · ARXIV 1903.05619 · DOI 10.1016/j.jctb.2022.01.006 · accessed Aug 26, 2026\n\nGraph theory · reconfiguration · graph coloring\n\n# Cereceda's Conjecture\n\nCollaboration beta\n\nFor each fixed degeneracy d and every palette of at least d+2 colors, can any two proper colorings be transformed into one another one vertex at a time using only quadratically many steps?\n\n\\text{diam}{C}_{k}\\left(G\\right)\\le {C}_{d}{n}^{2}\\phantom{\\rule{1em}{0ex}}(k\\ge d+2)\n\nKnown results and sources ↓\n\nImage: A dark six-vertex path uses coral, teal, split coral-and-ivory, plum, cyan, and amber disks, with an incomplete halo around the split focal disk.For each fixed degeneracy d and palettes of at least d+2 colors, Cereceda's conjecture asks for quadratic-length paths between proper colorings using one-vertex changes.On this pageProblem\n\nResearch problem\n\n## Exact mathematical statement\n\nFor a finite graph $G$, let $\\mathcal C_k(G)$ have the proper $k$-colorings of $G$ as vertices, with two colorings adjacent when they differ at exactly one graph vertex. Cereceda's conjecture asks whether, for every fixed $d$, there is a constant $C_d$ such that every $n$-vertex $d$-degenerate graph and every $k\\ge d+2$ satisfy\n\n$$\\operatorname{diam}\\mathcal C_k(G)\\le C_d n^2.$$\n\nThe retained source concentrates on $d=2$ and $k=4$, but its proposed sharp dynamic transition theorem is not yet proved.\n\nStatement boundary Research question, not proof evidence\n\nThe route map and retained computations do not by themselves establish this statement.\n\n## Problem at a glance\n\nFor each fixed degeneracy d and palettes of at least d+2 colors, proper colorings may change one vertex at a time; the conjecture asks for a quadratic-length route.\n\nCurrent mathematical picture\n\n## Where work on Cereceda's Conjecture stands\n\nOpen conjecture\n\nSelected route highlights from the mathematical source. This is not yet a complete mathematical inventory.\n\nUseful failure universal static mediator architecture\n\nThe cited source passage gives a seven-vertex adjacent transition with no relative forest certificate for any parking set. The separate ten-vertex static failure still has a source-reported legal ten-move dynamic path in which every vertex moves at most twice.\n\nRoute status · Narrowed route Main reduction Minimal-palette reduction\n\nThe current work states that it is enough to establish the conjecture at k=d+2 and defines the corresponding worst-case diameter D_d(n).\n\nEvidence posture · Source-reported route statement · dependencies incomplete Priority open bridge Give a canonical mathematical definition of the two-boundary profiles before introducing implementation-specific state or symmetry reductions.Task status · Ready to work on\n\n### Cereceda's Conjecture in numbers\n\n897 retained lines of mathematical investigation 897 in the current working snapshot\n\nArgument development\n    702 · 78%\n\nExplored or eliminated routes\n    40 · 4%\n\nComputational analysis\n    64 · 7%\n\nOpen obligations\n    38 · 4%\n\nDefinitions and setup\n    53 · 6%\n\n8selected mapped statements 2routes investigated 4open questions 4contribution-ready tasks\n\nHow this is measured\n\nThis measures retained mathematical investigation, not proximity to a proof. Code, data, logs, repeated text, operational instructions, and generated presentation copy are excluded.\n\nProposed byLuis Cereceda Proposed2007 Status checkedAug 26, 2026\n\nSource recordedAug 26, 2026\n\nRecommended next task\n\n## The known mathematical landscape\n\nContext collected Aug 26, 2026\n\nCurrent status Open conjecture\n\nThe general fixed-d minimum-palette quadratic diameter conjecture remains open in this bounded primary-source review. Published work gives an O(n^(d+1)) bound at k=d+2, linear diameter with substantially more colors, and stronger results for selected sparse classes, but not the full conjectured O_d(n²) statement.\n\n[3][4]\n\nOrigin Luis Cereceda · 2007\n\nCereceda's conjecture\n\n[1][3]\n\nExternal progress\n\n### What the literature has established\n\nSelected external milestones in reverse chronological order, with their evidence posture.\n\n  1. 2024\n\nPeer reviewed Cranston and Mahmoud advanced the recoloring-diameter program for sparse graph classes while continuing to treat Cereceda's quadratic conjecture as open.[4]\n  2. 2022\n\nPeer reviewed Bousquet and Heinrich proved an O(n^(d+1)) bound at the minimum palette and quadratic diameter above a larger palette threshold, leaving the general minimum-palette quadratic conjecture open.[3]\n\nSign in\n\nPrepared starting pointGive a canonical mathematical definition of the two-boundary profiles before introducing implementation-specific state or symmetry reductions.\n\nCereceda's Conjecture · ready to start\n\nStart this task\n\nMathematical updatesFollow this problem\n\nReceive an update when a route advances, an obstacle is clarified, or new evidence changes the mathematical picture.\n\nResearch contextPrepared context for any AI agent\n\nFor each fixed degeneracy d and every palette of at least d+2 colors, can any two proper colorings be transformed into one another one vertex at a time using only quadratically many steps?\n\n  * Exact question and boundaries\n  * Current routes and known obstacles\n  * What a useful result should report\n\nUse with an AI agent\n\nReturn mathematical workReturn what you or your agent found\n\nA proof attempt, partial advance, counterexample, useful failure, or corrected dependency can all move the shared frontier forward.\n\nProof attempt or partial result Supporting notes or data\n\nSubmit result\n\n  4. 4\n\nRecoloring sparse graphspeer reviewed result · Daniel W. Cranston, Reem Mahmoud · Journal of Graph Theory · 2024 · ARXIV 2208.02228 · DOI 10.1002/jgt.23064 · accessed Aug 26, 2026\n\n### Important qualifications\n\n  * Open status is a conservative inference from current peer-reviewed papers that continue to call the quadratic minimal-palette statement Cereceda's conjecture and prove weaker or class-specific bounds; a bounded search cannot establish the absence of every later claim.\n  * The original 2007 formulation is represented through Luis Cereceda's LSE doctoral thesis, while later papers provide the normalized degeneracy and palette formulation used here.\n  * The published bounds use varying palettes and graph classes; none is silently upgraded to the full fixed-d minimal-palette quadratic statement.\n  * No packet attachment, submitted URL, source-reported enumeration, source-reported verifier run, or model output was used as independent external status evidence.\n  * No statement-aligned formalization, certificate, or independently reproduced computation was established by the scoped search; empty resource lists do not assert nonexistence.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHaemers Conjecture in Spectral Graph Theory (https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/haemers-conjecture)\nciteturn5search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; # Haemers Conjecture in Spectral Graph Theory ... Seidel energy conjecture  | $E_S(G)\\ge 2n-2$E S​(G)≥2 n−2 for every $n$n-vertex graph  | Proved for graphs; hypergraph analogue fails in general (Akbari et al., 2019, Guterman et al., 16 Jun 2026) ... The van Dam–Haemers conjecture asserts that almost all graphs are DS: if $G$G 6 denotes the set of unlabelled $G$G 7-vertex graphs, then\n\n## 3. Laplacian toughness conjecture\n\nFor a finite, simple, undirected connected graph $t(\\Gamma)\\ge \\mu_2/(\\mu_n-\\delta)$t(Γ)≥μ 2​/(μ n​−δ)9, the toughness is\n\n$E_S(G)\\ge 2n-2$E S​(G)≥2 n−2 0\n\nwith the convention $E_S(G)\\ge 2n-2$E S​(G)≥2 n−2 1. If $E_S(G)\\ge 2n-2$E S​(G)≥2 n−2 2 is the Laplacian matrix, with Laplacian eigenvalues\n\n$E_S(G)\\ge 2n-2$E S​(G)≥2 n−2 3\n\nand $E_S(G)\\ge 2n-2$E S​(G)≥2 n−2 4 is the minimum degree, then Haemers conjectured that\n\n$E_S(G)\\ge 2n-2$E S​(G)≥2 n−2 5\n\nThis conjecture has now been proved (Greaves et al., 15 May 2026).\n\nThe theorem subsumes earlier spectral bounds, including those of Alon and Brouwer for regular graphs. In the $E_S(G)\\ge 2n-2$E S​(G)≥2 n−2 6-regular case, since $E_S(G)\\ge 2n-2$E S​(G)≥2 n−2 7, one obtains\n\n$E_S(G)\\ge 2n-2$E S​(G)≥2 n−2 8\n\nwhich is Brouwer’s conjectured bound, now recovered as a consequence of the Laplacian formulation (Greaves et al., 15 May 2026).\n\nThe proof is based on a cut decomposition. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA proof of Haemers' toughness conjecture (https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.15738)\nciteturn5academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Title: A proof of Haemers' toughness conjecture ... Date: Fri May 15 08:48:06 2026\nTitle: A proof of Haemers' toughness conjecture\nAuthors: Gary Greaves, Haoran Zhu\nDate: Fri May 15 08:48:06 2026\n\nWe prove that if $Γ$ is a connected graph with minimum degree $δ$ and Laplacian eigenvalues $0=μ_1<μ_2\\leqslant \\cdots \\leqslant μ_n$, then the toughness of $Γ$ is bounded below by $μ_2/(μ_n-δ)$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA polynomial version of Cereceda's conjecture - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0095895622000041)\nciteturn5search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; We complement Theorem 3 by proving the quadratic Cereceda's conjecture for planar bipartite graphs. ... Note that when the number of colours k is at least 6, then we have , since these graphs are 3-degenerate, and Theorem 3 already implies an upper bound on the diameter of if G is planar bipartite. ... Further work and open problems  Our result shows that we have polynomial upper bounds on the diameter of the reconfiguration graph, but the quadratic conjecture of Cereceda is still open, even for simple classes of graphs such as (triangle-free) planar graphs or 2-degenerate graphs.\nFurther work and open problems  Our result shows that we have polynomial upper bounds on the diameter of the reconfiguration graph, but the quadratic conjecture of Cereceda is still open, even for simple classes of graphs such as (triangle-free) planar graphs or 2-degenerate graphs. The question of Cereceda in [9] asks whether the diameter of is at most when . We were not able to determine if the coefficient in front of {n}^{2} has to be a function of d or not. As a consequence we decided to state the weaker possible version of the conjecture. Note that the constants which appear in Theorem 4, as well as the result for chordal graphs [5], and for bounded treewidth graphs [2] do not depend on d. A stronger conjecture would then be the following:\n\nQuestion 5 Stronger conjecture\n\nThere exists a constant C such that, for every d and every d-degenerate graph G the diameter of is at most as long as .\n\nAnother interesting question is the following: what is the evolution of the diameter when the number of colours increases. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCereceda's conjecture (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cereceda%27s_conjecture)\nciteturn5search15 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 months ago; In the mathematics of graph coloring, Cereceda’s conjecture is an unsolved problem on the distance between pairs of colorings of sparse graphs. ... He wrote that \"it remains to determine\" whether the diameter is polynomial for numbers of colors between these two extremes, or whether it is \"perhaps even quadratic\". ... *  Bonamy, Marthe; Bousquet, Nicolas; Feghali, Carl; Johnson, Matthew (2019), \"On a conjecture of Mohar concerning Kempe equivalence of regular graphs\", Journal of Combinatorial Theory, Series B, 135: 179–199, arXiv:1510.06964, doi:10.1016/j.jctb.2018.08.002, MR 3926265, S2CID 5465047\n\nAlthough Cereceda asked this question for a range of colors and did not phrase it as a conjecture, by 2018 a form of this question became known as Cereceda's conjecture. This unproven hypothesis is the most optimistic possibility among the questions posed by Cereceda: that for graphs with degeneracy at most d, and for (d + 2)-colorings of these graphs, the diameter of the space of colorings is O(n^{2}). If true, this would be best possible, as the space of 3-colorings of a path graph has quadratic diameter.\n## Partial and related results\n\nAlthough Cereceda's conjecture itself remains open even for degeneracy d = 2, it is known that for any fixed value of d the diameter of the space of (d + 2)-colorings is polynomial (with a different polynomial for different values of d). More precisely, the diameter is O(n^{d + 1}). When the number of colors is at least (3d + 3)/2, the diameter is quadratic.\n\nA related question concerns the possibility that, for numbers of colors greater than d + 2, the diameter of the space of colorings might decrease from quadratic to linear. Bousquet & Bartier (2019) suggest that this might be true whenever the number of colors is at least d + 3.\n\nThe Glauber dynamics is not the only way to change colorings of graphs into each other. Alternatives include the Kempe dynamics in which one repeatedly finds and swaps the colors of Kempe chains, and the \"heat bath\" dynamics in which one chooses pairs of adjacent vertices and a valid recoloring of that pair. Both of these kinds of moves include the Glauber one-vertex moves as a special case, as changing the color of one vertex is the same as swapping the colors on a Kempe chain that only includes that one vertex. These moves may have stronger mixing properties and lower diameter of the space of colorings. For instance, both the Kempe dynamics and the heat bath dynamics mix rapidly on 3-colorings of cycle graphs, whereas the Glauber dynamics is not even connected when the length of the cycle is not four.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWillem H. Haemers - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/author/willem-h-haemers)\nciteturn5search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Haemers ... 2026-09-30 v3 ... We discuss the question whether the existence of perfect matchings in a cubic graph can be seen from the spectrum of its adjacency matrix.\n\n## Papers by Willem H. Haemers\n\n31 paper(s) by this author · All BibTeX\n\n2026-09-30 v3\n\nWillem H. Haemers\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nOn perfect matchings, edge-colourings and eigenvalues of cubic graphs\n\nPublished • View Publication • BIB\n\nWe discuss the question whether the existence of perfect matchings in a cubic graph can be seen from the spectrum of its adjacency matrix. For regular graphs in general and for three edge-disjoint perfect matchings in a cubic graph (that is, an edge colouring with three colors) the answer is known to be negative. In the latter case, a few counter examples (found by computer) are known. Here we show that these counter examples can be extended to an infinite family by use of truncation. Thus we obtain infinitely many pairs of cospectral cubic graphs with different edge-chromatic number. For all these pairs both graphs have a perfect matching, and the mentioned question is still open. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA proof of Haemers' toughness conjecture | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2605.15738)\nciteturn5search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: last month; @misc{greaves2026proofhaemerstoughnessconjecture, title={A proof of Haemers' toughness conjecture}, author={Gary Greaves and Haoran Zhu}, year={2026}, eprint={2605.15738}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.15738}, }\nSubmitted 15 May 2026\n\n# A proof of Haemers' toughness conjecture\n\nGG\n\nGary Greaves\n\nHZ\n\nHaoran Zhu\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe prove that if $\\Gamma$Γ is a connected graph with minimum degree $\\delta$δ and Laplacian eigenvalues 0=\\mu_1&lt;\\mu_2\\leqslant \\cdots \\leqslant \\mu_n, then the toughness of $\\Gamma$Γ is bounded below by $\\mu_2/(\\mu_n-\\delta)$μ 2​/(μ n​−δ).\n\nView Paper\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{greaves2026proofhaemerstoughnessconjecture, title={A proof of Haemers' toughness conjecture}, author={Gary Greaves and Haoran Zhu}, year={2026}, eprint={2605.15738}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.15738}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA polynomial version of Cereceda's conjecture - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0095895622000041)\nciteturn5search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; 10.1016/j.jctb.2022.01.006 ... We also show that the 5-reconfiguration graph of planar bipartite graphs has quadratic diameter, confirming Cereceda's conjecture for this class of graphs.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTowards Cereceda’s conjecture (https://pure.royalholloway.ac.uk/files/35587343/recoloringsparse.pdf)\nciteturn5search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; cases, the diameter turns out to be quadratic in the number of vertices.) ... The foregoing pattern motivated Cereceda [7] to conjecture ... nau [6] have shown that for every d ≥1 and ϵ > 0 and every graph G with\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOne can almost never hear the shape of a digraph (https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.02165)\nciteturn5academia17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Date: Thu Apr  2 15:31:19 2026 ... Haemers conjectured that almost all graphs are determined by their spectra, whi